==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|tetrarchia}}, from Ancient Greek {{m|grc|τετραρχία}}, from the combining form of {{m|grc|τέτταρες}}, {{m|grc|τέσσαρες||four}} + {{m|grc|-αρχία}}.

===Pronunciation===
tet·rarch·y
* IPA: /'tɛtɹɑːkɪ/
* {{enPR|tĕträrkē}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|tetrarchies}}

# {{lb|en|politics|historical}} Co-rule by four rulers ({{m|en|tetrarch|tetrarchs}}); specifically, the system of such rule of the [[Roman Empire]] from 293-313 CE instituted by {{w|Diocletian}} to end the {{w|Crisis of the Third Century}}.
#* '''1996''', Sam Lieu, ''2: Constantine's "Pagan" Vision: The anonymous panegyric on Constantine (310), Pan. Lat. VII(6)'', Samuel N. C. Lieu, Dominic Montserrat (editors), ''From Constantine to Julian: Pagan and Byzantine Views: A Source History'', 2003, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=2ueJAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA66&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEI9wEwLA#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 66],
#*: Constantius was not born to the purple and Maximianus was the only original member of the First '''Tetrarchy''' from whom Constantine could satisfactorily derive his rule.
#* '''2010''', Roger Collins, ''Early Medieval Europe, 300-1000'', 3rd Edition, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ZukcBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA16&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIggIwLg#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 16],
#*: The abdication of Diocletian and Maximian in 305, whether long-planned or just the product of the senior emperor's recent ill health, created a second '''Tetrarchy''', in which the dominant figure should have been Galerius.
#* '''2015''', David M. Gwynn, ''Christianity in the Later Roman Empire: A Sourcebook'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=5aNOBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA15&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIiAIwLw#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 15],
#*: His imperial reorganization saw authority divided between four rulers, the '''Tetrarchy''', to oversee the recovery.
# {{lb|en|politics|historical}} Any of the portions of a previously intact realm divided into four parts, each separately ruled by a {{m|en|tetrarch}}; the division of a realm in such manner.
#* '''1830''', Edward Greswell, ''Dissertations Upon the Principles and Arrangement of a Harmony of the Gospels'', Volume 1, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ggUWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA229&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIbzAS#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 228],
#*: According to Josephus, Herod Agrippa, who succeeded Antipas in his '''Tetrarchy''', died in the seventh year of his reign;{{...}}.
#* '''1863''', James Hewitt, ''Scripture Geography'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=85gEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA66&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIhgMwRg#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 66],
#*: This district was included with Galilee in the '''tetrarchy''' of Herod Antipas.
#* '''1990''', Seth Schwartz, ''Josephus and Judaean Politics'', [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=N7MfAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA110&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEI_AEwLQ#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 110],
#*: After an adventurous career, Aristobulus' son Agrippa was appointed by Gaius, in 38 C.E., king of the former '''tetrarchies''' of Philip and Lysanias and was granted the praetorian ''ornamenta''. Soon after, he was awarded the '''tetrarchy''' of his uncle and brother-in-law Herod Antipas{{...}}.

====Usage notes====
Two '''tetrarchies''' are of particular historical note: the {{w|Herodian Tetrarchy}}, through which the {{w|Herodian kingdom}} of Judaea was split into separate states after the death of {{w|Herod the Great}}, and the '''{{w|Tetrarchy}}''' of Diocletian, through which rule of the Roman Empire was shared while the empire itself remained intact.

